oking more carefully at the analysis data in `code_status`, for release 01.1:\n- `rel01.1-uc001-cat`: specstatus: spec_complete, codestatus: not started, testdir: \"\", testfiles: 0\n\nAnd there's issue #296 for implementing cmd/cat (status: ready).\n\nThe constraints say to propose at most 1 task. Issue #296 is still open for implementation, so proposing a separate tests task would violate the no-duplication rule since the implementation hasn't even started yet. The tests would depend on the implementation being completed first anyway, and I'm limited to suggesting just one task for this release scope. only.\n\nHmm, but actually looking at this again - issue #296 has status \"ready\" which may mean it's ready to be worked on (not completed). If the implementation task is already tracked, maybe I should propose the test task.\n\nBut wait - looking at the test file situation: \"testdir: \"\", testfiles: 0\" - there are no test files. And the implementation issue #296 title says \"Implement cmd/cat concatenation and flag processing\" - this typically includes the test file in many project setups.\n\nActually, the planning constitution says:\n> Always split implementation and tests into separate tasks.\n\nSo if issue #296 is strictly an implementation task, there should be a separate test task. But I need to check - does the existing issue description (#296) include tests? The context only gives us the title, not the full description.\n\nGiven the constraints and the output format where dependencies reference indices within the current batch, I can't directly link to external issue #296. If I propose a test task, I'd set the dependency to -1 and note in the description that it depends on the cmd/cat implementation being complete. That seems like the right approach - proposing the differential tests as a separate task that builds on the existing implementation work. Since there's already issue #296 for implementing cmd/cat, and the planning constitution requires splitting implementation and tests, I should propose the test task. But the differential test for cmd/cat would require the binary to exist first, which creates a dependency issue.\n\nLooking at the constraint that I can only propose one task and shouldn't duplicate existing issues, and given that #296 already covers the use case for this release, I'm wondering if there's actually anything new to propose here. The issue title mentions both concatenation implementation and flag processing, so it might already encompass the tests despite the constitution's guidance to split them. I'm realizing I don't have the actual PRD content for the cat command in the project context, even though the specs are marked as complete. I know the requirement exists (rel01.1-uc001-cat) but without the specific PRD requirement IDs, I can't properly anchor the task as the planning constitution requires. I see that issue #296 already covers the cat implementation, so following the principle of splitting implementation from tests, I should propose the test task for rel01.1-uc001 instead of duplicating the existing work. Looking at the code status, the implementation hasn't started yet and there are no test files, so the question is whether issue #296 covers both implementation and tests or just implementation. Since the planning constitution calls for splitting those concerns and I can propose up to one task, I should propose a differential test task for the cat command if #296 is implementation-only. I'm realizing I need the actual PRD content to anchor to specific requirement IDs as the planning constitution requires, but I don't have access to it. I could list the PRD as required reading and let the stitch agent pull the specific requirements, then reference them generically in the task description.\n\nLooking at the existing issue #296, it doesn't include specific PRD IDs either, so maybe that's not strictly enforced here. I'll focus on defining what a differential test for cat should actually 